/*
# Splitter

The divider between two panes — a mail list and the message beside it, a sidebar and the
page — that a person drags to give one pane more room. **It is only the divider**: you
arrange the panes yourself, and the pane before it reads `value` for its size. Drag it,
or Tab to it and use the arrow keys; it keeps `value` inside `min`…`max` either way.

```declare
App [ width = 600, height = 300, fill = { provided("theme").surface },
    row: View [ width = 100%, height = 100%,
        layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = x ],
        side: View [ width = { app.row.split.value }, height = 100%, fill = { provided("theme").background },
            Text [ x = 16, y = 16, text = "Mailboxes" ] ],
        split: Splitter [ value = 200, min = 120, max = 400, accessibilityLabel = "Sidebar width" ],
        main: View [ width = { app.row.width - app.row.side.width - app.row.split.width }, height = 100%,
            Text [ x = 16, y = 16, text = "Message" ] ]
        ]
    ]
```

At rest it is a hairline in the theme's `line`; under the pointer, while dragged and while
focused from the keyboard it becomes a 2 px line in `accent`. Its own footprint along the
axis is the slim strip a pointer can catch (`thickness`), with the line centred in it.

The same value pattern as every control: standalone it owns `value`, as above; constrained
to your own state it needs `input` with it —
`Splitter [ value = { app.sideW }, input(v: number) { app.sideW = v } ]`.

## value
The size of the pane before the divider, in pixels. Standalone the splitter owns it;
constrained (`value = { app.sideW }`) it needs an `input` override with it, or the drag's
own delivery is refused against the constraint.

## min
The smallest `value` a drag or a key can deliver.

## max
The largest `value` a drag or a key can deliver.

## step
How far one arrow key moves the divider, in pixels. 10 by default.

## axis
Which way the divider moves: `x` (the default) is an upright divider between panes side by
side, moved left and right; `y` lies across between panes one above the other, moved up and
down. A bare token, as on `Divider`.

## thickness
The strip along `axis` that catches the pointer — 8 px by default. The line drawn inside
it is 1 px at rest and 2 px when active, so a wider strip makes it easier to catch without
making it heavier.

## input()
**Where the value goes**, continuously through a drag and once per key. Override it to
redirect the value (`input(v: number) { app.sideW = v }`); the default writes `value`.

## setTo()
Clamps a value into `min`…`max`, rounds it to a whole pixel, and delivers it through `input`
when it differs from `value`. Every change the splitter makes routes through it.

## toggle()
Collapses the pane before the divider to `min`, remembering where it stood; called again
from there, it restores that size. What Enter runs.

## home
The value the splitter had when it was built — what a double-click puts back.

## saved
Where `toggle()` restores to: the value the pane had when it was collapsed.

## active
Hovered, dragged, or focused from the keyboard — the state the accent line shows.

## dragging
True from pointer-down until the captured release. `pressed` will not do here: it goes
false when the pointer leaves the strip, and a drag must survive exactly that.

## _p0
**Internal.** The root-space pointer position along `axis` at the start of a drag.

## _v0
**Internal.** The value at the start of a drag, which the pointer's movement is added to.

## onInit
Records `home`. An override must call `super.onInit()`, or a double-click has nothing to
restore.

## onPointerDown
Claims focus, as every control does, and starts the drag.
An override calls `super.onPointerDown(e)`.

## onPointerMove
Moves the divider by the pointer's **root-space** movement since the press, so it keeps up
with a pointer that has left the strip.
An override calls `super.onPointerMove(e)`.

## onPointerUp
Ends the drag. A drag the browser takes back (`e.canceled`, a touch that became a scroll)
puts the value back where it started.
An override calls `super.onPointerUp(e)`: it is what ends the drag.

## onClick
Does nothing: a press on the divider that does not move is not a command.

## onDblClick
Restores `home`, the size the pane was built with.

## onKeyDown
The window-splitter keys: Left and Right (Up and Down on `axis = y`) move by `step` through
`accessibilityDecrement()` and `accessibilityIncrement()`; Home and End go to `min` and
`max`; Enter runs `toggle()`.

## accessibilityIncrement()
One `step` larger for the pane before the divider — what Right (or Down) runs, and what a
screen reader's increment calls.

## accessibilityDecrement()
One `step` smaller — what Left (or Up) runs.

## focusShape()
Hugs the 2 px line itself, stopping short of the ends so the ring stays inside the panes'
edges: on a focused divider the ring reads as a halo around the accent line.

## accessibilityRole
`separator`: a focusable one, with a value and a range.

## accessibilityLabel
Empty: a divider shows no words, so **the use site names it** for what it sizes — "Sidebar
width".

## accessibilityValue
Follows `value`, in pixels.

## accessibilityMin
Follows `min`.

## accessibilityMax
Follows `max`.

## width
`thickness` on an upright divider; the parent's width when it lies across.

## height
The parent's height on an upright divider; `thickness` when it lies across.

## span()
The length the divider runs across its axis: the parent's content height on an upright
divider, its content width on one that lies across.

## cursor
`col-resize` on an upright divider, `row-resize` on one that lies across.

## opacity
Disabled dims the divider to the theme's `disabledOpacity`.
*/

include [ "control.declare" ]

class Splitter extends Control [
    accessibilityRole = separator,
    accessibilityLabel = "",
    accessibilityValue = { value },
    accessibilityMin = { min },
    accessibilityMax = { max },

    value: number = 0,
    min: number = 0,
    max: number = 10000,
    step: number = 10,
    axis: Axis = x,
    thickness: number = 8,

    input(v: number) { value = v },
    setTo(v: number) {
        const n = Math.round(Math.max(min, Math.min(max, v)))
        if (n != value) input(n)
        },

    // where a double-click returns to, and where Enter restores a collapsed pane to

    home: number = 0,
    saved: number = 0,
    onInit() { home = value },

    toggle() {
        if (value > min) { saved = value; setTo(min) }
        else setTo(saved > min ? saved : home)
        },

    accessibilityIncrement() { if (!disabled) setTo(value + step) },
    accessibilityDecrement() { if (!disabled) setTo(value - step) },

    // the drag, by root-space movement along the axis

    dragging: boolean = false,
    _p0: number = 0,
    _v0: number = 0,

    onPointerDown(e: PointerEvent) {
        if (disabled) return
        focusVisible = false
        Focus.focus(this)
        dragging = true
        _v0 = value
        _p0 = axis == "x" ? e.rootX : e.rootY
        },
    onPointerMove(e: PointerEvent) { if (dragging) setTo(_v0 + (axis == "x" ? e.rootX : e.rootY) - _p0) },
    onPointerUp(e: PointerUpEvent) {
        dragging = false
        if (e.canceled) setTo(_v0)
        },
    onClick() { },
    onDblClick() { if (!disabled) setTo(home) },

    onKeyDown(e: KeyEvent) {
        if (disabled) return
        const less = axis == "x" ? "ArrowLeft" : "ArrowUp"
        const more = axis == "x" ? "ArrowRight" : "ArrowDown"
        if (e.key == less) accessibilityDecrement()
        else if (e.key == more) accessibilityIncrement()
        else if (e.key == "Home") setTo(min)
        else if (e.key == "End") setTo(max)
        else if (e.key == "Enter" && !e.repeat) toggle()
        else if (e.key != " ") super.onKeyDown(e)
        },

    span() {
        const p = this.parent as any
        if (p == null) return 0
        return p.contentBox(this.axis == "x" ? "height" : "width")
        },
    width = { axis == "x" ? thickness : span() },
    height = { axis == "x" ? span() : thickness },
    cursor = { disabled ? "" : axis == "x" ? "col-resize" : "row-resize" },
    opacity = { disabled ? provided("theme").disabledOpacity : 1 },

    active: boolean = { !disabled && (hovered || dragging || focusVisible) },

    // the ring hugs the strip the line sits in

    focusShape() {
        const across = this.axis == "x" ? this.width : this.height
        const along = this.axis == "x" ? this.height : this.width
        const a = (across - 2) / 2
        const b = Math.min(6, along / 4)
        return this.axis == "x" ? ({ x: a, y: b, w: 2, h: along - 2 * b, rad: 1 })
                                : ({ x: b, y: a, w: along - 2 * b, h: 2, rad: 1 })
        },

    line: View [ x = center, y = center, pointerEvents = "none",
        width = { classroot.axis == "x" ? (classroot.active ? 2 : 1) : parent.width },
        height = { classroot.axis == "x" ? parent.height : (classroot.active ? 2 : 1) },
        cornerRadius = { classroot.active ? 1 : 0 },
        fill = { classroot.active ? provided("theme").accent : provided("theme").line } ]
    ]
